 ## Overview
-MELON is a differentiable inverse rendering method that jointly optimizes a Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) and camera poses from a set of unposed images [S1]. It solves the problem of local minima in the low-dimensional $SO(3)$ latent space by introducing a "modulo loss" that maps the encoder's output to a quotient space, effectively replicating the latent variables to match the object's rotational symmetries [S1]. Unlike adversarial approaches such as GNeRF, MELON uses an encoder-decoder architecture with amortized inference, allowing it to perform ab-initio reconstruction without pose initialization or pre-trained CNNs [S1]. Does not require a discriminator [S1].
+MELON is a differentiable inverse rendering method that jointly optimizes a Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) and camera poses from a set of unposed images [S1]. It solves the problem of local minima in the low-dimensional $SO(3)$ latent space by introducing a "modulo loss" that maps the encoder's output to a quotient space, effectively replicating the latent variables to match the object's rotational symmetries [S1]. Unlike adversarial approaches such as GNeRF, MELON uses an encoder-decoder architecture with amortized inference, allowing it to perform ab-initio reconstruction without pose initialization or pre-trained CNNs [S1]. Does not require a discriminator (unlike GNeRF) [S1]. Builds on [Camera Pose and Extrinsic/Intrinsic Optimization](12051-camera-pose-and-extrinsic-intrinsic-optimization.md). Siblings include [Camera Pose and Parameter Optimization](7611-camera-pose-and-parameter-optimization.md), [Time-dependent NeRF with Continuous Camera Motion](12399-time-dependent-nerf-with-continuous-camera-motion.md), [Camera Preconditioning](12545-camera-preconditioning.md), [VMRF](12630-vmrf-view-matching-neural-radiance-fields.md), [SPARF](12705-sparf.md), [NeRF Pose and Appearance Optimization](13113-nerf-pose-and-appearance-optimization.md), and [Test-Time Two-Stage Optimization](13119-test-time-two-stage-optimization.md).
 
